import Mathlib

/-!
# Is there a faithful transitive locally finite action of the modular group?

Source: https://mathoverflow.net/questions/194207
Credit: @AgentsAlex

The question (MathOverflow 194207): is there a faithful transitive action of
`G = PSL₂(ℤ)` on `ℤ` such that the orbits under each `g ∈ G` are finite?

The one answer on the page shows that `PSL₂(ℤ)` has a transitive action on a countably infinite
set in which every element has finite orbits (pass to a free subgroup of finite index, then to a
quotient that is an infinite finitely generated torsion group, and induce back up). That action
factors through a proper quotient, so it is not faithful; the faithful case asked here is not
settled on the page.

Formalization notes:
* `PSL(2, ℤ)` is Mathlib's `Matrix.ProjectiveSpecialLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℤ`, i.e.
  `SL₂(ℤ)` modulo its center `{±1}`.
* An action of `G` on `ℤ` is given as a group homomorphism `ρ : G →* Equiv.Perm ℤ`;
  faithful means `ρ` is injective, transitive means every `x` can be moved to every `y`.
* "The orbits under each `g` are finite" is read as: for every `g` and every `x`, the orbit of
  `x` under the cyclic group generated by `g`, `{(ρ g) ^ n x | n : ℤ}`, is finite.
* The statement is the existence asked for; a disproof answers the question negatively.
-/

namespace OMA.S_mo_194207

open scoped MatrixGroups

/-- MathOverflow 194207: a faithful transitive action of `PSL₂(ℤ)` on `ℤ` in which every element
has finite orbits. -/
theorem main :
    ∃ ρ : PSL(2, ℤ) →* Equiv.Perm ℤ,
      Function.Injective ρ ∧
      (∀ x y : ℤ, ∃ g : PSL(2, ℤ), ρ g x = y) ∧
      ∀ (g : PSL(2, ℤ)) (x : ℤ), (Set.range fun n : ℤ => (ρ g ^ n) x).Finite := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_mo_194207
